✅ Interfaces vs Abstract Classes in Java

| Property                  | **Interface**                                                                 | **Abstract Class**                                                  |
| ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Keyword**               | `interface`                                                                   | `abstract class`                                                    |
| **Method Implementation** | Until Java 7: abstract only. Java 8+: `default` + `static`. Java 9+: `private` | Can have both **abstract** and **concrete** methods                |
| **Multiple Inheritance**  | A class can **implement multiple** interfaces                                 | A class can **extend only one** class (abstract or concrete)        |
| **Constructor**           | ❌ Cannot have constructors                                                    | ✅ Can have constructors (used by subclasses via `super(...)`)      |
| **Variables/Fields**      | Only constants → implicitly `public static final`                             | Can have instance variables with any access modifier                |
| **Access Modifiers**      | Abstract methods are `public`. Also `default` / `static` / `private` (Java 9+) | Can use `public`, `protected`, `private`, package-private          |
| **Usage Purpose**         | Define a **contract** / **capability**                                        | Define a **base type with shared code** for related classes         |
| **Instantiation**         | ❌ Cannot be instantiated                                                      | ❌ Cannot be instantiated                                            |
| **Extends/Implements**    | Class **implements** interface; interface can **extend** other interfaces     | Class **extends** abstract class; abstract class can **implement** interfaces |

────────────────────────────────────────
❌ Can you create an object of an abstract class?
No. An abstract class is incomplete (may have abstract methods with no body), so Java does not allow `new AbstractClass()`.
Subclasses that implement all abstract methods **can** be instantiated.


────────────────────────────────────────
✅ When to Use an Interface

1) You want to define a contract
"Any class that implements this must provide these methods."
Think of it as a job description — what to do, not how.

```java
interface Printable {
    void print(); // rule only — no body
}

class Invoice implements Printable {
    @Override
    public void print() {
        System.out.println("Printing invoice...");
    }
}

class Document implements Printable {
    @Override
    public void print() {
        System.out.println("Printing document...");
    }
}
```

2) Unrelated classes need the same behavior
`Bird`, `Drone`, and `Airplane` can all fly, but are not in one inheritance tree:

```java
interface Flyable {
    void fly();
}
```

All of them can `implements Flyable`.


────────────────────────────────────────
✅ When to Use an Abstract Class

1) Share common code among related classes
Abstract class can hold shared logic + force subclasses to fill gaps:

```java
abstract class Animal {
    void sleep() {
        System.out.println("Sleeping...");
    }

    abstract void makeSound(); // each animal defines its own sound
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Bark");
    }
}

class Cat extends Animal {
    @Override
    void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Meow");
    }
}
```

2) You need constructors or instance variables
Interfaces cannot have instance fields or constructors; abstract classes can:

```java
abstract class Shape {
    int sides;

    Shape(int sides) {
        this.sides = sides;
    }

    abstract void draw();
}
```


────────────────────────────────────────
🧠 Quick decision table

| Situation                                  | Interface | Abstract Class |
| ------------------------------------------ | --------- | -------------- |
| Unrelated classes sharing behavior         | ✅ Yes     | ❌ Poor fit     |
| Shared code among related classes          | Partial (`default` methods) | ✅ Best fit |
| Need constructors / instance variables     | ❌ No      | ✅ Yes          |
| Need multiple inheritance of type          | ✅ Yes     | ❌ No           |
| Define capability/contract only            | ✅ Best    | Possible, but heavier |


🎤 Quick interview traps
1) Interface after Java 8 is **not** always 100% abstract (`default` / `static` / `private`).
2) Abstract class **can** have constructors — still cannot be instantiated directly.
3) “Shared code only in abstract class” is outdated — interfaces can share code via `default` methods.
4) A class can implement many interfaces, but extend only **one** class.
